WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:17.437
The water kept rising, and Peter was alone in the lagoon on Marooners' Rock.

2
00:00:17.438 --> 00:00:24.007
All he could hear were the mermaids calling good-bye to the moon as they swam to their bedrooms in the sea.

3
00:00:24.008 --> 00:00:29.459
Soon the water completely covered the rock and rose above Peter's ankles.

4
00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.942
As he waited for his doom, Peter watched the only thing moving on the water.

5
00:00:34.943 --> 00:00:40.006
At first he thought it might be a piece of paper, a part of the kite.

6
00:00:40.007 --> 00:00:44.408
But then he noticed it was fighting the current and getting closer.

7
00:00:44.409 --> 00:00:50.523
Suddenly, he realized it was a bird, and it was trying to reach him.

8
00:00:50.524 --> 00:00:56.643
The bird was still sitting on her nest, but she was guiding it using her wings as paddles.

9
00:00:56.644 --> 00:01:00.542
By the time she reached Peter, she was very tired.

10
00:01:00.543 --> 00:01:06.221
She was prepared to share her nest with him, even though her eggs were still in it.

11
00:01:06.222 --> 00:01:12.250
Her motherly instincts told her that Peter was only a child, and she wanted to save him.

12
00:01:12.251 --> 00:01:16.275
The bird called out to Peter and he called out to her.

13
00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:21.031
But they couldn't understand each other's language.

14
00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:27.088
"I want you to swim to my nest," she called in her language.

15
00:01:27.089 --> 00:01:31.321
"I am too tired to bring it any nearer."

16
00:01:31.322 --> 00:01:35.277
"What are you quacking about?" asked Peter.

17
00:01:35.278 --> 00:01:45.575
"I want you to swim to me," called the bird, as clearly as she could.

18
00:01:45.576 --> 00:01:49.484
Peter spoke slowly, thinking that would help.

19
00:01:49.485 --> 00:01:56.352
"What . . . are . . . you . . . quacking . . . about?" he called back.

20
00:01:56.353 --> 00:02:03.278
"You silly boy! Why don't you listen to me?" said the annoyed bird.

21
00:02:03.279 --> 00:02:07.790
Both were exhausted and close to losing their tempers.

22
00:02:07.791 --> 00:02:11.300
But the bird meant to save Peter if she could.

23
00:02:11.301 --> 00:02:15.871
With one last try, she pushed the nest against the rock.

24
00:02:15.872 --> 00:02:19.182
Then she flew up, circling overhead.

25
00:02:19.183 --> 00:02:25.452
"Oh, now I understand what you're trying to do!" Peter exclaimed.

26
00:02:25.453 --> 00:02:29.482
He grabbed the nest and waved his thanks to the bird.

27
00:02:29.483 --> 00:02:33.256
She waited to see what he would do with her eggs.

28
00:02:33.257 --> 00:02:36.624
Peter pulled out the two large white eggs.

29
00:02:36.625 --> 00:02:41.190
Then he remembered Starkey's hat, which was still on his head.

30
00:02:41.191 --> 00:02:44.709
Yes, the hat was watertight.

31
00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:49.244
With its wide brim, the hat would easily float on the water.

32
00:02:49.245 --> 00:02:54.163
Carefully, he placed the eggs in it and put the hat in the water.

33
00:02:54.164 --> 00:02:56.312
It stayed afloat.

34
00:02:56.313 --> 00:03:02.465
Peter then got into the nest and took off his shirt to make a sail.

35
00:03:02.466 --> 00:03:07.672
The bird saw what Peter had done and immediately went after the hat.

36
00:03:07.673 --> 00:03:12.677
Happy to have her eggs back, she sat down on them to keep them warm.

37
00:03:12.678 --> 00:03:17.087
Then she drifted in one direction as Peter drifted in the other.

38
00:03:17.088 --> 00:03:22.634
She cried at him and he crowed at her—in shared respect.

39
00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:28.567
When Peter landed near home, he put the nest where the bird could easily find it again.

40
00:03:28.568 --> 00:03:31.540
But the bird never came back for it.

41
00:03:31.541 --> 00:03:35.354
She was perfectly happy with the hat for her eggs.

42
00:03:35.355 --> 00:03:40.764
The broad brim was just right for the young chicks when they finally hatched.

43
00:03:40.765 --> 00:03:45.249
It provided an excellent springboard for them while they were learning to fly.

44
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:52.683
And when the hat finally fell apart with age, the bird built her new nests in the shape of a hat ever after.

45
00:03:52.684 --> 00:03:58.131
When Peter finally reached the underground house, everyone was happy.

46
00:03:58.132 --> 00:04:03.706
After being carried here and there by the kite, Wendy had also just arrived home.

47
00:04:03.707 --> 00:04:11.151
Every boy had his own tale of adventure, and they all spoke at once—except for the twins.

48
00:04:11.152 --> 00:04:18.271
But the biggest topic of conversation was that they were already many hours late for bed.

49
00:04:18.272 --> 00:04:24.780
"To bed, to bed, boys!" Wendy cried, and they all went off to bed.

50
00:04:24.781 --> 00:04:31.942
The next day, she gave out bandages to everyone, so they could all put their arms into slings.

51
00:04:31.943 --> 00:04:43.130
As everyone knows, one cannot win in battle without some serious injuries.
